  "id": "Compound::DB00667 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Histamine Phosphate is implicated in the relationship with Cyp2d6 through a shared pathway involving the binding of specific compounds to the enzyme. The evidence suggests that Histamine Phosphate causes Paresthesia Of Limbs and Welts, both of which are mediated by Protriptyline and Pethidine, respectively. These compounds bind to Cyp2d6, indicating a potential mechanism by which Histamine Phosphate may influence the enzyme's activity. The lowest common ancestor of these pathways is a P-Block Molecular Entity and a Pnictogen Molecular Entity, suggesting a broader molecular context in which Histamine Phosphate interacts with Cyp2d6. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Histamine Phosphate in modulating Cyp2d6 activity through the action of these compounds, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
